📅 Day 18 – Collections Framework (List & Set)

Full Stack Web Development with Java Internship

Today’s session focused on the Collections Framework in Java, particularly the List and Set interfaces. The mentor explained how collections provide dynamic data storage, overcoming the size limitations of arrays.

🔹 Topics Covered
1️⃣ Introduction to Collections Framework

Collections are used to store and manipulate groups of objects.

Unlike arrays, collections are dynamic in size.

Located in java.util package.

🔹 2️⃣ List Interface

The List interface allows ordered collection of elements.

Implementations Discussed:
✅ ArrayList

Maintains insertion order

Allows duplicate elements

Fast for retrieval

Slower for insertion/deletion in middle

✅ LinkedList

Maintains insertion order

Allows duplicates

Faster insertion and deletion

Slower random access

🔹 3️⃣ Set Interface

The Set interface stores unique elements (no duplicates allowed).

Implementation Discussed:
✅ HashSet

Does not maintain insertion order

Automatically removes duplicate elements

Faster performance for add/remove operations

🔹 Practical Implementation

Implemented programs using ArrayList to store and retrieve elements.

Used HashSet to observe automatic removal of duplicate values.

Practiced basic operations:

add()

remove()

contains()

Iteration using loop

🎯 Learning Outcomes

Understood difference between arrays and collections.

Learned features of List and Set interfaces.

Gained clarity on ArrayList, LinkedList, and HashSet.

Practiced common collection operations.

Strengthened understanding of dynamic data handling.